##### Commit several writes atomically

```typescript
await db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  await tx.orm.public.Tag.create({ label: 'tx-commit-a' });
  await tx.orm.public.Tag.create({ label: 'tx-commit-b' });
});
// both tags exist now
```

content.text:
`updateMany` applies one `data` payload to every matched row, so it can't set different `order` values per group — you need two separate `update` calls wrapped in a transaction so they commit atomically.

**Current API (Prisma 8+)** — `db.transaction(callback)`:
```ts
await db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  await tx.orm.public.TodoGroup.update({ where: { id: groupA.id }, data: { order: 2 } });
  await tx.orm.public.TodoGroup.update({ where: { id: groupB.id }, data: { order: 1 } });
});
```
(*apps/docs/content/docs/orm/reference/transactions-and-runtime.mdx*, "db.transaction(callback) > Examples > Commit several writes atomically")

**If you're on Prisma 7 or earlier**, use `prisma.$transaction([...])` with two `update` calls instead — it was removed in Prisma 8 (*apps/docs/content/docs/orm/fundamentals/writing-data.mdx*, "Common mistakes > Passing an array of queries to a transaction").

